   raw c is the UNTUNED model's error. The kakaki's lip model starts about
   2.5 semitones flat and the jet about a third of a semitone sharp: that is
   what the self-tuning is for, and why the waveguides report it.

-- Scene 5: the tuner is not its own witness --
   Three notes, one per engine family, re-measured with an instrument that
   shares no code with the engine: an eight-pole lowpass, then the time
   between the first and last rising zero crossing across 0.7 s, each
   crossing located between samples. The zokra, not the mezwed: the mezwed's
   two chanters BEAT, and a beat null flips the phase and adds a crossing.
   guitar   asked 303.600 Hz, independently measured 303.600 Hz = -0.000 cents
  [ok]   guitar is in tune by an instrument that shares no code with the tuner
   zokra    asked 480 Hz, independently measured 479.937 Hz = -0.228 cents
  [ok]   zokra is in tune by an instrument that shares no code with the tuner
   imzad    asked 379.500 Hz, independently measured 379.516 Hz = 0.073 cents
  [ok]   imzad is in tune by an instrument that shares no code with the tuner

-- Scene 6: the engines are DISTINCT, by their physics --
   Twenty names on one sound would pass every scene above. These cannot.
   2nd / 3rd harmonic: mezwed (single reed, cylinder) 0.015   zokra (double reed, cone) 0.178
  [ok]   the cylinder suppresses its even harmonics -- H2 below half of H3
   the cone's even content is 11.782 times the cylinder's
  [ok]   the cone does not suppress them -- five times the cylinder's even content
   loudness at 0.9 s over 0.3 s: imzad (bowed) 1.030   guitar (plucked) 0.427
  [ok]   the bow SUSTAINS the note
  [ok]   the pluck DECAYS it
   fundamental / mode (2,1): dum (centre) 17.534   tak (rim) 0.739
  [ok]   DUM, struck at the centre, is led by the fundamental
  [ok]   TAK, struck at the rim, is led by the upper mode
   2.5 kHz over fundamental: bendir 0.039   darbouka 0.000
  [ok]   the bendir BUZZES where the darbouka does not -- five times the high band

-- Scene 7: a pitch that moves -- the kalangu, and the bow --
   The talking drum is squeezed from 150 to 220 Hz over 0.8 s. Its pitch is
   read early and late, each against where the glide should be at the
   middle of that window -- a glide is not a note, and the tolerance is 30
   cents, stated here before it was measured.
   early window: expected 162.770, read 161.763   late: expected 202.740, read 201.323
  [ok]   the early window is where the glide should be
  [ok]   and so is the late one
   the glide should rise 380.148 cents between the window centres; it rose 378.758
  [ok]   and the pitch ROSE by the glide's own amount between them, to 30 cents
  [ok]   a plucked string REFUSES to glide -- it holds one pitch
   this instrument cannot glide: a pluck and an FM voice hold one pitch

-- Scene 8: a sample played at another pitch -- how a recording becomes an instrument --
  [ok]   the rate is accepted
   a 220 Hz note played at 2^(7/12) reads 329.614 Hz, -0.069 cents from the fifth above
  [ok]   the sample sounds a fifth higher, to 2 cents
  [ok]   a rate of 0 is REFUSED
  [ok]   rate 1 is bit-identical to a rate never set -- 0 samples differ

